{"schema":1,"fm":{"schema":1,"id":"notes-vault-cleanup-track","kind":"workflow","title":"Notes collection cleanup track","description":"Rescues a cluttered notes collection in five gated steps - inventory, a target structure and tag rules, pruning and merging, linking with hub notes, and a maintenance routine.","category":"note-taking","version":"1.0.0","status":"incubating","stage":["review","design","maintain"],"role":["individual","researcher","writer","student"],"stack":["obsidian","notion"],"requires":["none"],"inputs":["notes","text"],"output":["plan","table","docs","checklist"],"risk":"read-only","invocation":"user","effort":"deep","interaction":"interactive","model_tier":"frontier","reasoning":"recommended","level":"intermediate","depth":"specific","tags":["vault-cleanup","tag-sprawl","digital-declutter","hub-note","maintenance-routine"],"pairs_with":{"prompts":["audit-notes-vault","design-note-tagging-scheme","merge-overlapping-notes","write-map-of-content","plan-notes-backup-and-export"],"personas":["notes-system-coach"]},"args":[{"name":"inventory","description":"A list of your notes (paths or titles, with dates, sizes and link counts if your app shows them) and your current tag list. A sample of a few hundred is fine.","type":"text","required":true},{"name":"purpose","description":"What the collection is for now, which app you use, and what is going wrong.","type":"text","required":true}],"steps":[{"id":"inventory","file":"steps/01-inventory.md","stage":"review","gate":"approve"},{"id":"target-structure","file":"steps/02-target-structure.md","stage":"design","gate":"approve"},{"id":"prune-and-merge","file":"steps/03-prune-and-merge.md","stage":"maintain","gate":"approve"},{"id":"connect","file":"steps/04-connect.md","stage":"maintain","gate":"approve"},{"id":"routine","file":"steps/05-routine.md","stage":"maintain","gate":"none"}],"authorship":"ai-assisted","authors":["gabrielanhaia"],"last_reviewed":"2026-10-11","changelog":[{"version":"1.0.0","note":"First version."}]},"body":"Cleans up a cluttered notes collection the way an experienced knowledge coach would, pausing after each step for the person's approval. The inventory shapes the target structure, the structure decides what to prune and merge, the cleaned set is then linked, and a routine keeps it tidy. Each step builds only on approved earlier steps.\n\n<inventory>\n{{inventory}}\n</inventory>\n<purpose>\n{{purpose}}\n</purpose>\n\nThroughout: work only from the notes and tags the person gives; never invent notes, dates or counts. Prefer reversible actions: archive before delete, rename before restructure, and make a full backup before step 3. Never suggest deleting notes that may hold credentials, legal, health or financial records; flag them for the person. Do not state app menu paths or plugin names as current fact. If the person asks to skip the pauses, confirm once that later steps will rest on unchecked choices; if they agree, run the remaining steps in one reply and mark each assumption.","steps":[{"id":"inventory","file":"steps/01-inventory.md","stage":"review","gate":"approve","text":"# Step 1: Inventory\n\nSee what is there before changing anything.\n\n1. If the inventory is not a list of notes, ask for one and stop.\n2. Summarise: rough counts by folder or type, age spread, tag count, and the two or three biggest problems.\n3. Group candidates, quoting titles: duplicates or overlaps, empty or \"Untitled\" notes, stale drafts, orphans, notes from a finished chapter of life, sensitive records to leave alone.\n4. Say which checks you could not do because dates, sizes or links were missing.\n\nStop and ask the person to confirm the problems and the purpose before designing the structure."},{"id":"target-structure","file":"steps/02-target-structure.md","stage":"design","gate":"approve","text":"# Step 2: Target structure\n\nDesign where things will live, kept as small as possible.\n\n1. Propose at most about six top-level folders for the stated purpose, plus an Archive.\n2. Write tag rules: what a tag is for, when to use a link or a property instead, and a short tag list grouped as type, status and topic.\n3. Map every existing folder and tag to its new place, a link, a property, or \"drop\".\n4. Set note title rules for the main note types.\n\nStop and ask the person to approve the structure and the mapping before anything is moved."},{"id":"prune-and-merge","file":"steps/03-prune-and-merge.md","stage":"maintain","gate":"approve","text":"# Step 3: Prune and merge\n\nReduce clutter safely.\n\n1. Remind the person to make a full backup or export first, and wait for a yes.\n2. Give an action table for the step 1 candidates: keep, rename, move, merge into (named note), archive, or delete-after-check.\n3. For each merge, list the notes, what to keep from each, and conflicts the person must resolve.\n4. Order the work into 30-minute sessions, biggest gain first.\n\nStop and ask the person to approve the actions, or report back after the first session."},{"id":"connect","file":"steps/04-connect.md","stage":"maintain","gate":"approve","text":"# Step 4: Connect\n\nMake the cleaned collection easier to find your way around.\n\n1. Ask for the titles, and a line each, of the notes in the two or three most-used areas.\n2. Propose a hub note (map of content) for each area: sections, notes in order with exact titles as links, and a few words on each.\n3. Suggest the ten strongest links between notes, with the relation (supports, contradicts, example of) and the sentence to add.\n4. List gaps: notes the hubs suggest are missing.\n\nStop and ask the person to approve the hubs before setting up the routine."},{"id":"routine","file":"steps/05-routine.md","stage":"maintain","gate":"none","text":"# Step 5: Routine\n\nKeep it from drifting back.\n\n1. Write a weekly ten-minute sweep: empty the inbox, file or link new notes, fix new tags against the tag list.\n2. Write a monthly check: unused tags, new duplicates, stale drafts to archive.\n3. Write a yearly check: archive finished projects, test a backup restore, review the folder list.\n4. Put the rules from step 2 on one page the person can keep at the top of the collection."}]}
